EEPROM - the BIOS chip

Every computer runs the code on its EEPROM when it starts. A crafted
EEPROM holds the Lua BIOS, which boots /init.lua from a drive.

  Code area:  4096 bytes of Lua, run in the sandbox at boot.
  Data area:  256 bytes; by convention the boot drive's address.
  Label:      shown on the item (24 characters).
  Read-only:  `flash --lock` locks code and label forever.

The flash program:
  flash bios.lua      write bios.lua to the EEPROM
  flash -r out.lua    save the current code to a file
  flash -c            size, checksum, read-only state
  flash -l "My BIOS"  set the label
  flash -d            show the data area (boot address)

The eeprom component (component.eeprom):
  get() set(code) getData() setData(data) getLabel() setLabel(l)
  getSize() getDataSize() getChecksum() isReadOnly()
  makeReadonly(checksum)
set, setData, setLabel and makeReadonly take one tick each.

Writing your own BIOS: the code runs with the globals component,
computer, unicode and the Lua standard library. There is no OS:
use component.invoke and computer.pullSignal directly. Minimal:

  local gpu = component.list("gpu")()
  local screen = component.list("screen")()
  component.invoke(gpu, "bind", screen)
  component.invoke(gpu, "set", 1, 1, "Hello from my BIOS")
  while true do
    local name, _, char = computer.pullSignal()
    if name == "key_down" then computer.beep(440 + char) end
  end

If the code errors or returns, the computer stops and shows the
error. A broken EEPROM can be reflashed from another computer, or
replaced. Full guide: docs/EEPROM.md in the mod's source.
